{"id": "sevt_019gEKCjFLyik2oiM89exGWJ", "content": [{"text": "Confirmed. The evidence is decisive:\n\n**Timeline**\n- `14:31:18` checkout deploy `a3f9c21` (\"refactor order summary builder\")\n- `14:32` p99 breaks from ~67 ms \u2192 347 ms, climbing to ~2\u20133.5 s and staying there\n- `14:40` error_rate rises from ~0 to ~10% (downstream saturation as slow queries pile up)\n\n**Mechanism** \u2014 classic N+1. The diff replaced one batched query with a per-order query inside a loop:\n- Before: `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=order_ids)` \u2192 **2 DB queries/request** (orders + items), measured at 14:30\n- After: `for order in orders: OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=order.id)` \u2192 **16 DB queries/request** at 14:35 (one per open order)\n- The first `order_items WHERE order_id = ?` per-row query in the log appears at `14:31:18.144Z` \u2014 the exact deploy instant \u2014 and none exist before it.\n\nThe `log.debug` line Alice added is what makes it visible in the log; the real damage is the query-per-iteration. No index or upstream dep is involved \u2014 purely the batch\u2192per-row regression.\n\n> **Root cause:** `a3f9c21` \u2014 replaced a single batched `OrderItem` query with a per-order query inside the loop, turning each checkout-summary request into an N+1 (2\u219216 DB round-trips), which saturated the DB and drove checkout p99 from ~65 ms to multiple seconds. Revert/re-batch to fix.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:40:37.632324Z", "type": "agent.message"}
